{"kind":"request","text":"Archive budget: settle read reservations to the bytes received (from the measurement 3763c6d8 and the checker's point 1 in b6fd83e3).\n\nWhy: Room A's plan pass admits 2,894,539 metadata of which 1,835,008 are seven fixed 262,144 ceilings reserved before reads (four target births and two item queries in archive-target.ts:59-73, one source birth in apps/room/archive.ts:88-95), and a further 104,448 are six pin SELECT ceilings (archive-pin.ts:46,51; archive-driver.ts:179-180; archive-budget.ts:155-163) and 12,715 receipt-bound frontier payload ceilings for 5,194 actual bytes (journal-v2-frontier.ts:28-38,94-107). With the finish forecast and the 65,536 margin the pass is 4,673,364 against 4,194,304. The ceilings, not R7, are why the row does not fit. Lowering ceilings per site would be tuned by observation and break on a larger genesis; the general change is in the budget.\n\nDesign (decided):\n1. ArchiveBudget gains a two-phase charge: `reserve(cost)` charges the ceiling as spend does today and returns a handle; `settle(handle, received)` reduces the metadata charge to the bytes actually received (and inputWork in the same proportion), never below zero and never above the reservation. A reservation that is never settled stays charged in full, so the pass can never exceed the limit mid-flight. The limit check (admission and the finish forecast) counts settled charges plus outstanding reservations.\n2. Every read site that charges a ceiling before a bounded read adopts reserve/settle: the seven 262,144 sites, the six pin SELECT ceilings, the frontier payload ceilings. Sites that charge actual sizes stay on spend. No new ceiling values; no site-specific tuning.\n3. The archive-pass-diagnostic-v1 row reports both the reserved and the settled metadata for the pass, so a tail shows the difference.\n4. Nothing else changes: no plan-path reorder, no finish-formula change, no change to the row or pin bounds.\n\nConditions:\n- Check every budget dimension at its charge sites (metadata, inputWork, calls, nodes, time), and compute admission with the head's own code, per the checker's standing rule.\n- Witness W1: the retained Room A numeric pass (evidence 2026-10-27-archive-rooma-33387, the measurement's reconstruction inputs) replayed through the head's admission code with settled charges, reporting the settled total and whether it fits 4,194,304 with the margin; paired control: the same replay with settlement disabled reproduces 4,673,364 exactly.\n- Witness W2: a fixture pass where a reservation is never settled (the read throws) stays charged in full and the pass stops at the limit as today.\n- Witness W3: a settle above the reservation or below zero is refused and recorded.\n- Ordinary gate; exact-head checker review; PD-2 note and manifest under the evidence rules. The planner deploys dap-grow after landing and captures the next Room A pass by tail; the head's note names the expected settled total for that pass.\n- Priority: archive work first, before the R14 documents head and the standing release items.","body":{"conditions":"every budget dimension checked at its charge sites and admission computed with the head's own code; W1 Room A replay with settled charges and the unsettled paired control reproducing 4,673,364; W2 unsettled reservation stays charged; W3 bad settle refused; ordinary gate; exact-head checker review; PD-2 note and manifest; planner deploys and captures","target_head":"716360793fcbba627e621382cd3b312022ff47db","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}